\section{Lags and differences}{

Inside the formula, \code{lag(x, k)}, \code{lead(x, k)} and
\code{diff(x, lag, differences)} are taken within each individual, never
across individuals. Shifts step through the sorted distinct values of
the time index, so \code{lag(x, 1)} is the value at the previous observed
wave. With gaps in the time index this differs from plm: for waves
coded 2010, 2012, 2014, \code{lag(x, 1)} at 2012 is the 2010 value in
panglm, while \code{plm::lag()} (which shifts by the numeric time value)
gives \code{NA}. To obtain plm's behaviour, keep a row for every period,
with \code{NA} outcomes for the missing waves. \code{\link[=pangmm]{pangmm()}} uses the same rule.
The first \code{k} waves of each individual get \code{NA} and leave the
estimation sample. \code{diff()} follows base R's \code{diff(x, lag, differences)};
plm's third argument of \code{diff()} is \code{shift}, not \code{differences}.
The same rule applies in \code{\link[=paniv]{paniv()}}, \code{\link[=panqr]{panqr()}} and \code{\link[=panfmm]{panfmm()}}.
}

\section{Validation}{

Each estimator is checked against an established implementation of the same
model, on the same data, in the package's validation suite on GitHub and in
the package tests:
\itemize{
\item pooled models: \code{stats::lm()}, \code{stats::glm()}, \code{MASS::glm.nb()},
\code{plm::plm()} and fixest, with \code{sandwich} robust and cluster covariances;
\item fixed effects: \code{plm::plm()}, \code{fixest::feols()}, \code{fixest::fepois()},
\code{fixest::fenegbin()}, \code{pglm::pglm()}, \code{glm()} with individual dummies, and
\code{survival::clogit()} for the conditional and two-way logit;
\item between estimator: \code{plm::plm(model = "between")};
\item random effects: \code{plm::plm()} and \code{pglm::pglm()} (random intercept),
\code{lme4::lmer()}, \code{lme4::glmer()} and glmmTMB (random intercept and slopes),
and GLMMadaptive (adaptive quadrature with random slopes);
\item ordered outcomes: \code{MASS::polr()}, \code{ordinal::clm()}, \code{ordinal::clmm()}, and
a blow-up and cluster fit with \code{survival::clogit()} for the fixed-effects
ordered logit;
\item Tobit: \code{AER::tobit()} and censReg; beta: \code{betareg::betareg()} and glmmTMB;
gamma: \code{stats::glm()} and glmmTMB.
}
}

\examples{
data(copd)

# pooled OLS on lung function (ignores the panel structure)
fit_pool <- panglm(fev1 ~ treatment + age + smoker + crp, data = copd,
                    index = c("id", "visit"), model = "pooling", family = "gaussian")
summary(fit_pool)

# fixed-effects ("within") estimator: only the time-varying regressor
# (crp) survives demeaning; time-invariant covariates like treatment
# or age are not identified by a fixed-effects estimator
fit_fe <- panglm(fev1 ~ crp, data = copd,
                  index = c("id", "visit"), model = "within", family = "gaussian")
coef(fit_fe)

# random-effects (Swamy-Arora) estimator
fit_re <- panglm(fev1 ~ treatment + age + smoker + crp, data = copd,
                  index = c("id", "visit"), model = "random", family = "gaussian")
coef(fit_re)

# fixed-effects Poisson on exacerbation counts
fit_pois <- panglm(exacerbations ~ crp, data = copd,
                    index = c("id", "visit"), model = "within", family = "poisson")
coef(fit_pois)

# correlated random effects (Mundlak) probit for hospitalization
fit_cre <- panglm(hospitalized ~ treatment + age + crp, data = copd,
                  index = c("id", "visit"), model = "random",
                  family = "probit", cre = TRUE)
summary(fit_cre)

# patient trajectories: random intercept and random slope of FEV1
# decline per visit, correlated
fit_rs <- panglm(fev1 ~ visit + treatment + age + smoker, data = copd,
                 index = c("id", "visit"), model = "random",
                 random = ~ visit)
summary(fit_rs)
fit_rs$varcomp

# two-way conditional logit: smoking across survey waves, with year
# shocks (e.g. a tobacco tax) absorbed by the time effects
set.seed(1)
smk <- expand.grid(wave = 1:5, id = 1:200)
smk$price <- rnorm(nrow(smk)) + 0.2 * smk$wave
smk$smoking <- rbinom(nrow(smk), 1, plogis(rnorm(200)[smk$id] - 0.5 * smk$price +
                                               c(0, 0.2, -0.3, -0.6, -0.8)[smk$wave]))
fit_2w <- panglm(smoking ~ price, data = smk, index = c("id", "wave"),
                 model = "within", family = "binomial", effect = "twoways",
                 vcov = "cluster")
summary(fit_2w)
fit_2w$time_effects

# nonlinear effect through a natural spline: linearity test and curve
fit_ns <- panglm(fev1 ~ splines::ns(crp, 3), data = copd,
                 index = c("id", "visit"), model = "within", vcov = "cluster")
lineartest(fit_ns, "crp")
plot(effectcurve(fit_ns, "crp"))
}
